db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076078 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076078 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076078 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0007cb31-26a9-5baa-bb56-3315e50f92d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076079 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076079 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076079 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5423d064-430d-5df3-844e-c5b95a86e210" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076080 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076080 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076080 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "573eb60d-c9a8-5e53-86cf-c5b203b832ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076081 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076081 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076081 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "57aa2166-1627-5c3f-b2d3-eb4cdfbc2479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078179 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078179 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\nI. $2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: \\ldots$\n\nII. $5, \\: 2, \\: -1, \\: -4, \\: \\ldots$\n\nIII. $2, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: \\ldots$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078179 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eba75ca0-7f78-531f-96c4-b772b5785828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078179 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence.\n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence I is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2.$\n\n![](images/17/17933a1cabd8d487dd1607f7ae7064d6e186e3454a52497977f866150c491f25.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence II is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-3.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d9a41035215deb27d0578123a859d9b492b505d79b0a0a71f62c822942298ca.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence III is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](images/2b/2b860338be6ff8876c1368ed5f08162096873fa9992a9e88d7ff545752a5bd36.png)\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II are arithmetic sequences." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078180 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14381" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078180 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the $8$th term of the following arithmetic sequence?\n\n$$\n4, \\: 7, \\: 10, \\: 13, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078180 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "510e7581-c8f5-51b0-931b-02f6e926e5f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078180 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/ac/ac878a3d258fa19641d983863580af953bbea593b49bffff16f2f09eb7b6ab2d.png)\n\nWe notice that the sequence is an arithmetic sequence with a common difference of \n\n$$\nd=7-4=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we can calculate each term in the sequence by adding $3$ to the previous term. \n\nWe will continue listing terms of the sequence until we get to the $8$th term. Listing the terms up to the $8$th term, we have:\n\n![](images/e5/e536739b28db7d0f221967ce8729eee502a29f80caec8e1c39f61a9cfe4a6459.png)\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is $25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078194 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078194 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the common difference of the following arithmetic sequence?\n\n$$\n7,\\: 2,\\: -3,\\: -8,\\: \\ldots\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078194 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae7753b6-b58d-57c5-849e-c887e7d1601e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078194 #edb/ref 1074 "The common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence is the difference between any two consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common difference for our sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =2-7 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078420 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078420 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078420 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078420 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078420 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078420 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078420 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Common Difference" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078420 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078420 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ac161f82-668f-5e67-b8d8-c2f6a438c7a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078421 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078421 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078421 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078421 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying an Arithmetic Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078421 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078421 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "839431c8-b292-5ed9-b549-2d81b12df45b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078422 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078422 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078422 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078422 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078422 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078422 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Terms of an Arithmetic Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078422 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078422 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "73efb24d-e21f-5999-b545-2e3539440735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078683 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078683 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078683 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078683 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078683 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078683 #edb/ref 1045 "Arithmetic Sequences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078683 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078683 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078683 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078683 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a7455b77-2b32-5022-8104-55f7d28d3665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078883 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078883 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078883 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078883 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\nI. $6,10,14,18,...$\n\nII. $4,8,16,32,...$\n\nIII. $87,74,61,46,...$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078883 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d14404fc-deb2-5ffc-b0b6-821e376bc0d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078883 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence.\n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence I is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=4.$\n\n![](images/bb/bbb6555443ed4fdf7f29da4571d421b833fb21e2556a440d821bbd4e4b986923.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence II is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](images/64/6408dd510dd59615c69c063897a6ff483c20bc485d73164e74c2f57dacc4dce8.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence III is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](images/24/246210488611eed52df8d3e777e296bff30889c7dd8122d082c5d4a6bb6d7ad6.png)\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence I is an arithmetic sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079288 #edb/ref 1061 147303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77248861-cfe7-52c2-a064-19e52e2c8319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079289 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079289 #edb/ref 1061 147304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079289 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74f48168-99d1-5fe5-b8e4-1ba057d4761b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079289 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079290 #edb/ref 1061 147308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "83aa323f-ab48-5ff6-bc1e-9bc8b30f4a09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079291 #edb/ref 1061 147311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3ac2040-039f-52d7-af0b-3310ec3c3324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079726 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 9,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that the difference (or \"jump\") between two consecutive numbers is always the same. \n\n![](images/5a/5a4433d95492b9895793bc497fce887e3a39986a4f0c686ebe8a5d9b54d4f3dc.png)\n\nA sequence where the jump never changes is called an **arithmetic sequence.** \n\nWe call this jump the **common difference**, and represent it using the letter $d.$ In this particular case, the common difference is $d=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079726 #edb/ref 1065 8907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079726 #edb/ref 