519476 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab00a963-1cd0-5605-a266-0b494ab59620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519497 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519497 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519497 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519497 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186519372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519497 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d52aac11-c9ed-51ba-b011-6d52463e90a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519527 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519527 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519527 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following arithmetic sequence.\n\n$$\n-22,\\,-31,\\,-40,\\,-49,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe common difference of this arithmetic sequence is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "201f78ad-e932-54f4-94ee-8dd6f6a1154d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519527 #edb/ref 1074 "The common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence is the difference between any two consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common difference for our sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-31-(-22) \\\\  & =-31+22 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519560 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519560 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\nI. $-2,\\,-4,\\,-6,\\,-8,...$\n\nII. $-2,\\,-9,\\,-16,\\,-23,...$\n\nIII. $-2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,16,...$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8dd21bc7-cc1c-556a-83c5-25089927d9b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519560 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence. \n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence I is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-2.$\n\n![](images/c5/c599604e6630b0dbf74d646f93b6330b5d310f35bfbce7b008f16d1686534c77.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence II is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-7.$\n\n![](images/b3/b35cc27bfa6f7fe2171529a0c84e9ab8d33c122e932512fae1b06fe21cc9bc94.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence III is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=6.$\n\n![](images/9a/9aa43107c2b5c975450590f4bc126273b45ddc72f1acfd3d35eadff0313ded1b.png)\n\nIn conclusion, sequences I, II, and III are arithmetic sequences." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519574 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519574 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67189" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519574 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519574 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the common difference for the following arithmetic sequence?\n\n$$\n-5,\\,-12,\\,-19,\\,-26,\\,…\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519574 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8cf8516-d7bc-5fa0-86d7-e74e836777c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519574 #edb/ref 1074 "The common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence is the difference between any two consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common difference for our sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-12-(-5) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519579 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519579 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519579 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519579 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the third term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $23$ and the common difference is $-7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519579 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7af1a90-ae65-5ea4-82bf-4487562443e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519579 #edb/ref 1074 "We will list terms of the sequence until we get to the third term. \n\nWe start with the first term $23,$ and then we calculate each subsequent term by subtracting $7$ from the previous term:\n\n![](images/86/86fe86315966ba57e674e652510c2814ab94f0cf8869656c9fa3b7c934801b9f.png)\n\nTherefore, the third term is $9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519586 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519586 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519586 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the fourth term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $1$ and the common difference is $-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519586 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4919d08-0fd6-56c9-962f-b38905295542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519586 #edb/ref 1074 "We will list terms of the sequence until we get to the fourth term. \n\nWe start with the first term $1,$ and then we calculate each subsequent term by subtracting $2$ from the previous term:\n\n![](images/2f/2f8e4976e5ea05c0d238959425d4d2d5ece23d4bfae844e533e9eb484f9de18a.png)\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is $-5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
